;;; DRAFT -- delete!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; Prose seeded by the local model
;;; (mlx-community/Mistral-Small-3.2-24B-Instruct-2506-4bit via
;;; scratch/pairs/draft_records.py). Raw model output for this
;;; verb is kept verbatim at drafts/_raw/delete-bang.slat.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/delete-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "delete!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(delete! x lst [=]) -> list"
  :summary "Every element of lst equal to x removed, by unlinking. The argument is destroyed."
  :explanation "delete! is remove! with the predicate fixed to a comparison against one object. Every element matching x goes, not just the first -- this is a filter, not a find-and-remove. The optional third argument is the comparison, and its ARGUMENT ORDER is the detail that catches people: it is called as (= x element), with the object you are deleting FIRST. The suite proves this at list.sld:576-582, where (delete! 5 (list 1 48 2 5 37) <) yields (1 2 5) -- 48 and 37 went because (< 5 48) and (< 5 37) are true, and 5 itself stayed because (< 5 5) is false. Read as (< element 5) the answer would have been different, so this is not a stylistic point. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. The default comparison is equal?, not eqv? and not eq?, so delete! on a list of lists compares contents."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 1 2 1 3))\n(display (delete! 1 xs))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the comparison is called (= x element) -- x FIRST.\n(define xs (list 1 48 2 5 37))\n(display (delete! 5 xs <))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; the default comparison is equal?, so contents are compared, not identity.\n(define xs (list (list 1) (list 2) (list 1)))\n(display (delete! (list 1) xs))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed. list.sld:576-582 asserts every cell of the result came from the argument."
             "The optional comparison is called as (= x element), with x -- the value being deleted -- as the FIRST argument. list.sld:576-582 pins this with (delete! 5 x <) yielding (1 2 5)."
             "ALL matching elements are removed, not just the first."
             "The default comparison is equal?, so structurally equal values are deleted even when they are not the same object."
             "The result is eq? to the argument only when the first element does not match.")
  :drawbacks ("The comparison argument order is backwards from what most callers assume and there is no way to detect the mistake with a symmetric predicate."
             "Using a non-symmetric comparison is legal and almost always a mistake in disguise."
             "Like remove!, it leaves a fragment rather than a truncation.")
  :usecases ("Purging a known sentinel value out of a private list."
             "Removing all occurrences of a retired identifier from a self-owned index."
             "Set-like difference against a single element where the list has one owner.")
  :related ("delete"
             "remove!"
             "delete-duplicates!"
             "filter!"
             "member")
  :learn (:concept "Fixed-object deletion and comparison argument order"
          :prerequisites ("delete" "remove!" "equal?" "member")
          :progression "Call delete with a non-symmetric comparison such as < and predict the answer before running it. Getting that prediction wrong once is how the argument order gets learned.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:576-582, which also pins the comparison argument order; signature derived from the delete record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 1 3))\n(display (delete! 1 xs))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 1 3))\n(define b (list 1 2 1 3))\n(display (equal? (delete 1 a) (delete! 1 b)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 48 2 5 37))\n(display (list 'x-comes-first (delete! 5 xs <)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants every occurrence of one value gone from a list they own. Candidates: delete (fresh list, source intact), (remove! (lambda (e) (equal? x e)) lst) (same effect, and writing the lambda is where the argument order stops being a trap because you chose it), or delete! (shortest, source destroyed, and the comparison order is now the library's choice rather than yours). The explicit lambda is worth considering purely because it makes the order visible."
     :explanation "eng: every matching element is gone and the list variable is unusable. math: delete! agrees with delete for every x, list and comparison. cs: the comparison receives x first, which the < example makes impossible to misread."
     :meta "A convenience verb hides a decision the explicit form makes you state. Sometimes that decision is one you want to see.")
    (:eng "When a shorthand hides an argument order, the longhand is not more verbose, it is more honest."
     :math "delete(x, l, =) = remove(lambda e. =(x, e), l); the order is part of the specification."
     :cs "A symmetric comparison hides the bug, so the test must use a non-symmetric one.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Removing every occurrence of one value from a sequence by unlinking its cells."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (delete! 1 (list 1 2 1 3)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a bunch of keys with every copy of one key taken off" :code "(define keys (list 'front 'back 'front 'shed))\n(display (delete! 'front keys))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a stock list with a discontinued line withdrawn" :code "(define stock (list 'a 'discontinued 'b 'discontinued))\n(display (delete! 'discontinued stock))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, every cell whose element matches is bypassed. Taking every copy of one key off a bunch removes all of them, not the first one you find, and the ring closes up. Withdrawing a discontinued line from a stock list is the same act on a catalogue. All three share a removal that is exhaustive rather than positional."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever \"remove that value\" means all of them, which is nearly always what a person means.")
    (:eng "People mean all of them. A verb that removes the first is the one that needs the special name."
     :math "Deleting a value is set difference against a singleton, and it is idempotent on the pure form."
     :cs "Exhaustive removal is one pass; first-match removal is one pass with an early exit, and they are different verbs.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define r (delete! 1 (list 1 2 1 3)))\n(display (list 'result r))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (none-left? x xs) (not (member x (delete x xs))))\n(display (list (none-left? 1 (list 1 2 1)) (none-left? 9 (list 1 2))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 48 2 5 37))\n(define r (delete! 5 xs <))\n(display (list 'result r 'proves-order (equal? r (list 1 2 5))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "delete! removes every element e of lst for which (= x e) is true, where = defaults to equal? and is called with x as its first argument. The result preserves order, is built from lst's own cells, and is equal? to (delete x lst =)."
     :invariant "forall x, proper lst, comparison =. equal?(delete!(x, lst, =), delete(x, lst, =)) and no element e of the result satisfies =(x, e) and every cell of the result is a cell of lst"
     :meta "The pattern is exhaustive value removal. What would falsify the claim: a surviving match, a reordering, an allocated cell, or -- the one a symmetric test cannot see -- a comparison called as (= element x). The < program is included precisely because it is the only one of the three that can detect the argument order.")
    (:eng "One of these three programs can detect the argument-order bug. The other two cannot. Know which is which."
     :math "The post-condition \"no element satisfies the comparison\" is what makes deletion exhaustive by definition."
     :cs "Choosing a non-symmetric comparison for the test is a deliberate technique, not a curiosity.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (withdraw! stock line) (delete! line stock))\n(display (withdraw! (list 'a 'x 'b 'x) 'x))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (difference xs ys)\n  (fold (lambda (y acc) (delete y acc)) xs ys))\n(display (difference (list 1 2 3 4) (list 2 4)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (dedupe xs) (delete-duplicates xs))\n(display (dedupe (list 1 2 1 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "delete! removes every element matching one value, by unlinking."
     :emergent "eng: a withdrawal step in a catalogue. math: folding delete over a second list is set difference, and it is written with the pure verb because the fold reads its accumulator again on every step -- which the destructive form has already eaten. cs: delete-duplicates is the same machinery pointed at the list itself rather than at an external value, which is why the two records sit next to each other."
     :meta "The family is set-like removal. What emerges is a boundary that keeps recurring: fold and recursion need their accumulator intact, so the destructive verbs live at the leaves of a computation, never in its spine.")
    (:eng "Destructive verbs belong at the leaves. Any accumulator that is read again needs the pure form."
     :math "Set difference is a fold of deletions; the fold is what forces the purity."
     :cs "Deduplication is deletion aimed inward, and it inherits every property of deletion including the comparison order.")
  )
)
